      ],
      "message": "memusage: model glibc and musl allocators in MallocUsage\n\nThe previous formula was measured on glibc 2.19 and overestimates the\nsize of glibc chunks by 16 bytes for half of all request sizes, ignores\nglibc's minimum chunk size, and has no notion of mmapped allocations.\n\nModel the allocators actually used by Linux builds:\n- glibc (ptmalloc2, all Linux release builds): chunks of\n  max(MINSIZE, round_up(size + sizeof(size_t), MALLOC_ALIGNMENT)) bytes,\n  with chunks of 128 KiB or more mmapped as whole pages. This matches\n  glibc 2.43 exactly on x86_64, i386, aarch64, armhf, armel, riscv64,\n  ppc64, ppc64le, ppc, s390x, loongarch64, alpha, hppa, m68k, sh4 and\n  sparc64 (over 300000 request sizes each).\n- musl (mallocng, used by the Alpine CI job): a table of the steady-state\n  cost per slot for each of its 48 size classes, including group headers\n  and out-of-band metadata, which matches RSS measurements of musl 1.2.5\n  within 0.2%.\n\nOther platforms (macOS, Windows, BSDs) keep the previous estimate until\nthey are measured.\n\nAlso count the hash that libc++ stores in every std::unordered_map and\nstd::unordered_set node. The previous MallocUsage() overestimated small\nallocations enough to hide this, but with the accurate glibc model,\npool_tests/memusage_test fails with libc++ (it finds that the pool\nallocator uses more memory than std::allocator, as the std_map nodes are\ncounted as 24 instead of 32 bytes).",
