      "message": "test: compare MallocUsage against measured allocator behaviour\n\nAdd a unit test that measures how much memory malloc() actually consumes\nand fails if MallocUsage() is more than a factor 1+1/16 below, or more\nthan a factor 1+1/8 above, the measured usage. The upper bound is looser\nbecause MallocUsage() may include out-of-band allocator metadata that the\nmeasurement does not see (up to 1/8 for musl's smallest size class).\n\nBy default, a random set of sizes up to 4 MiB is tested: for every power\nof two 2^b, 16 >> (b / 4) (but at least one) sizes in [2^b, 2^(b+1)), so\nsmall sizes are tested more densely than large ones. Every mismatching\nsize is reported on its own line, with its measured usage.\n\nWhen the ALL_SIZES constant is set to true, the test instead\ndetermines the usage of every size up to 4 MiB (searching for the\nboundaries between size classes, assuming usage does not decrease with\nsize), reports mismatching size ranges, and prints the results in a form\nmeant for writing MallocUsage() code, e.g. for glibc on x86_64:\n\n  1-24: 32\n  25-131048: round_up(size + 8, 16)\n  131049-4194280: round_up(size + 24, 4096)\n\nThis takes about 25 seconds with glibc, and 5 with musl. In this mode, glibc's mmap\nthreshold is pinned to its default, as glibc otherwise raises it whenever\nan mmapped block is freed, which would make the results depend on the\norder of the measurements.\n\nThe usage of a size is measured as the smallest distance between\nneighbouring blocks (in address order), among up to 16384 live\nallocations of that size, that accounts for at least 10% of all pairs.\nThis is robust against a fragmented heap (the test binary's heap holds\nover a thousand regularly spaced free holes at startup, which dominate\nsmaller samples) and against allocators that place blocks at varying\noffsets within their slots (musl).\n\nLarge blocks are often given page-granular memory, with the block at an\noffset within it that varies from block to block (musl cycles it), which\nmakes neighbouring blocks slightly closer than the memory they use. So\nfor distances of a page or more, the test also looks at the longest run\nof neighbours whose distances are within a page of that distance. If all\nof them lie within less than a page of a grid whose spacing is a multiple\nof the page size, that spacing is the usage. Blocks that are really\nspaced by something else (e.g. glibc's 4080-byte chunks) drift away from\nany such grid over the run. Without this, musl's output in the ALL_SIZES\nmode has two lines per page above its mmap threshold, one of them 16\nbytes too low; with it, the output is:\n\n  1-12: 16\n  13-156: round_up(size + 4, 16)\n  157-188: 192\n  ... (one line per size class) ...\n  104829-131052: 131072\n  131053-4194284: round_up(size + 20, 4096)\n\nSanitizer builds are skipped, as MallocUsage() does not model their\nallocators.",
